{# ── Chapter: FE Depth ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="fe-depth">
  <h2>Knowing which image is "above the fold" isn't always obvious</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      "Above the fold" depends on viewport size, and a hero image that's fully visible on desktop might be partially or fully below the fold on a narrow phone screen with a tall header and nav. When in doubt, treat any image that could plausibly be the LCP element on your most common viewport as above-fold, rather than guessing from one layout.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      Use Chrome DevTools' Performance panel or the Lighthouse report to identify the actual LCP element on a real page load — it names the specific element, so you don't have to guess which image (or text block) it picked.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      Don't mark more than one or two images <code>fetchpriority="high"</code> per page — it's a relative priority hint, and if everything is high priority, the browser's scheduler has no signal left to act on. Reserve it for the actual LCP candidate, not every image that feels important.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

